# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Structured content for The RAG Stack cheat sheet (extracted from the source workbook builder)."""

SECTIONS = [{'id': 'tokenization', 'num': '01', 'eyebrow': 'Where text meets math', 'title': 'Tokenization — Atoms of Language', 'core': 'Before an LLM can do anything, raw human text must be chopped into computable numerical segments called tokens. Every downstream capability — vocabulary, pricing, context limits — is built on this first cut.', 'defs': [('Word-level splitting', 'Splits by whole words. Natural to read, but vocabulary explodes and it breaks on typos, slang, or new words.'), ('Character-level splitting', 'Splits into single letters/symbols. Solves the unknown-word problem but produces very long sequences with weak semantics.'), ('Subword-level splitting', 'The modern standard — splits rare words into reusable root fragments (e.g. "un-happi-ness"), balancing vocabulary size and sequence length.'), ('BPE (Byte Pair Encoding)', 'Starts from single characters, iteratively merges the most frequent adjacent pair. Backbone of GPT-family tokenizers.'), ('WordPiece', 'Merges the pair that maximizes training-data likelihood. Popularized by BERT.'), ('SentencePiece', 'Treats whitespace as a normal character (shown as "_"). Essential for languages without spaces, e.g. Japanese.')], 'tables': [{'caption': 'Tokenization pipeline (text -> model input)', 'headers': ['Step', 'What happens', 'Example'], 'rows': [['1. Raw text', 'User input string', '"Learning is fun"'], ['2. Subword split', 'Tokenizer breaks text into chunks', '["Learn","ing"," is"," fun"]'], ['3. Vocabulary mapping', 'Each chunk -> integer ID', '[4512, 273, 318, 1109]'], ['4. Dense embedding', 'IDs -> continuous vectors for the model', '']]}], 'facts': ['1 word ≈ 1.33 tokens on average · 1 token ≈ 0.75 words (~4 characters)', 'Optimized LLM vocabularies run 32,000-100,000 unique tokens', '"undergraduate" -> ["under","grad","uate"] — BPE lets the network relate it to other academic words'], 'quirks': ['Spelling blindspot — models see tokens, not letters. Counting the "r"s in "strawberry" fails because it\'s read as ["straw","berry"].', 'Multilingual inequity — vocabularies are English-heavy, so non-English text splits into far more tokens, which is slower and pricier.', 'Context ceiling — a static token limit; exceed it and the model "forgets" earlier context.', 'Spacing effect — a leading space is often baked into the token itself (" hello" is not "hello"); tiny punctuation changes can shift output.'], 'qa': [('Why not just split on whitespace?', 'Word-level tokenization creates an unbounded vocabulary and breaks completely on typos, slang, or unseen words — subword methods like BPE keep vocabulary compact while still handling novel words by decomposing them.'), ("What's the practical difference between BPE and WordPiece?", "Both are subword merge algorithms; BPE merges the most frequent adjacent pair, while WordPiece merges the pair that most improves the training-data likelihood. BPE is GPT's default; WordPiece is BERT's."), ('Why does SentencePiece matter for non-English text?', "It treats the space character itself as a token-worthy symbol, so it doesn't assume word boundaries exist — critical for languages like Japanese that don't use spaces.")]}, {'id': 'context-windows', 'num': '02', 'eyebrow': 'Working memory', 'title': 'Context Windows', 'core': "The context window is an LLM's short-term working memory: the total token budget it can attend to at once, shared between the prompt (instructions, history, pasted docs) and the completion being generated.", 'defs': [('Context window', "Fixed token budget an LLM can see at once — think 'a fixed number of sticky notes on a desk.'"), ('Truncation', "When space runs out, the earliest tokens are dropped — the model 'forgets' them."), ('KV cache', 'Stored key/value representations for every past token; long contexts consume massive GPU RAM to keep this cache alive.'), ('Lost-in-the-middle effect', 'Even multi-million-token windows attend unevenly — a U-shaped curve that favors the start (primacy) and end (recency), missing details buried in the middle.')], 'tables': [{'caption': 'RAG vs. stuffing the full context window', 'headers': ['', 'RAG (retrieval)', 'Long-context stuffing'], 'rows': [['Mechanism', 'Searches an external DB, fetches ~3-5 relevant chunks', 'Pastes hundreds of pages into a 1M+ token window'], ['Trade-off', 'Fast, cheaper, highly accurate', 'Simple to build, but higher latency & cost']]}], 'facts': ['Doubling context from 4k to 8k tokens costs roughly 4x more compute (self-attention scales quadratically)', 'Context windows have grown ~1,000x in a few years', 'Life cycle of a prompt: Tokenize -> Position-map (rotary embeddings) -> Self-attention -> Predict next token'], 'quirks': [], 'qa': [('Why does doubling the context window quadruple compute?', 'Self-attention computes a relationship score between every pair of tokens, so cost scales with the square of sequence length — 2x tokens means 4x token-pairs.'), ('When would you pick RAG over just using a huge context window?', "When latency/cost matters, when the corpus exceeds even a 1M-token window, or when you need to cite/ground specific sources — RAG fetches only what's relevant instead of paying to process everything every call."), ("What is the 'lost in the middle' problem and why does it matter for RAG?", 'Models attend best to the start and end of context and worst to the middle, so if your retrieved chunk lands mid-prompt in a long context, the model may under-use it — argues for reordering retrieved chunks or keeping context short.')]}, {'id': 'embeddings-fundamentals', 'num': '03', 'eyebrow': 'Meaning as geometry', 'title': 'Vector Embeddings — Fundamentals', 'core': "A vector embedding is an array of floating-point numbers encoding the semantic meaning of data — 'GPS coordinates of meaning.' Items with similar meaning land close together in that space, regardless of shared characters.", 'defs': [('Cosine similarity', 'Measures the angle between two vectors — near 1.0 means highly related, near 0 means unrelated.'), ('Vector arithmetic / semantic algebra', 'Embeddings support analogy math: V(King) - V(Man) + V(Woman) ≈ V(Queen).'), ('Multimodal embeddings', 'Not limited to text — images, audio, and code can share the same coordinate space as their text descriptions.'), ('High-dimensional space', 'Real embedding models operate in 768-1,536+ dimensions; each dimension captures a subtle nuance (tone, syntax, sentiment, domain).')], 'tables': [], 'facts': ['Keyword matching fails on synonyms: "cat" vs. "feline" share no characters but nearly identical meaning', 'Homonym problem: "bank" (river) and "bank" (financial) are identical strings with different meanings — embeddings disambiguate via context', 'Real-world uses: semantic search, recommendation systems, fraud/anomaly detection, clustering & classification'], 'quirks': [], 'qa': [("Why can't exact keyword matching find 'affordable places to live' when a listing says 'Cheap Apartments for Rent'?", 'No shared characters, so string matching returns nothing — but both phrases embed to nearby vectors because embeddings encode meaning, not spelling.'), ('What does cosine similarity actually measure, and why not just use vector distance?', 'The angle between two vectors, ignoring magnitude — this makes it robust to differences in vector length/scale, which raw Euclidean distance is sensitive to.'), ('Give an example of vector arithmetic capturing a real relationship.', "V(King) - V(Man) + V(Woman) ≈ V(Queen) — the 'royalty' direction stays constant while the 'gender' direction shifts, showing the space encodes composable semantic features.")]}, {'id': 'rag-overview', 'num': '04', 'eyebrow': 'Open-book exam', 'title': 'RAG Overview', 'core': "A standard LLM answers from memory and can hallucinate. RAG gives it an 'open book': look up the exact source, then answer. Four building blocks make the pipeline work.", 'defs': [('Chunking', "Break a large document into smaller pieces because you can't feed a 500-page PDF to an LLM at once."), ('Embeddings + vector DB', 'Each chunk becomes a vector, stored so related topics cluster together and can be searched by distance.'), ('Similarity search', "The query is embedded too; cosine similarity finds the 'closest' chunks (smallest angle = highest similarity)."), ('Metadata', "Digital tags (page, author, date, category) that let you filter precisely — e.g. 'Revenue' only within '2023 Reports.'")], 'tables': [{'caption': 'RAG design trade-offs', 'headers': ['Category', 'High-performance choice', 'Trade-off'], 'rows': [['Latency', 'Smaller chunks / skip re-ranking', 'Faster, but lower answer quality'], ['Cost', 'In-house storage / open-source models', 'Cheaper, but needs setup & ops'], ['Accuracy', 'Semantic chunking + ~10-chunk context', 'Best quality, but higher token cost']]}], 'facts': [], 'quirks': [], 'qa': [('In one sentence, why does RAG reduce hallucination?', 'It forces the model to ground its answer in retrieved source text instead of relying purely on parametric memory.'), ('Name the four core building blocks of a RAG pipeline.', 'Chunking, embeddings + vector database, similarity search, and metadata filtering.')]}, {'id': 'chunking', 'num': '05', 'eyebrow': 'Slicing the corpus', 'title': 'Chunking Strategies', 'core': 'Chunking divides source documents into coherent text segments before embedding. Get it wrong and you either fragment facts or dilute them — this is the highest-leverage RAG design decision.', 'defs': [('Fixed-size chunking', 'Splits by a strict character/token count (e.g. 512 + 64 overlap). Cheap and deterministic, but breaks mid-sentence. Good for prototypes.'), ('Sentence/paragraph chunking', 'Splits on linguistic boundaries (., ?, !, blank line). Preserves syntax but produces variable-length chunks.'), ('Recursive character chunking', 'Tries an ordered hierarchy of separators ["\\n\\n","\\n"," ",""] — paragraph splits first, falls back only when needed. The industry default.'), ('Semantic chunking', 'Splits at meaning/topic shifts, not character counts — dynamic chunk boundaries.'), ('Parent-child (small-to-big)', "Small chunks (~128-256 tokens) are searched; each links to a larger parent chunk (~1024-2048 tokens) that's actually passed to the LLM."), ('Contextual retrieval', "An LLM prepends 1-2 explanatory sentences to each chunk before embedding, fixing the 'orphan chunk' problem."), ('Structure-aware chunking', 'Parses document syntax directly: code AST (keeps functions/classes intact), Markdown header hierarchy, table-preserving units.'), ('Agentic chunking', 'An LLM reads line-by-line and decides chunk boundaries dynamically based on topic shift.'), ('Graph-linked chunking (GraphRAG)', 'Extracts entities and builds knowledge-graph edges connecting related chunks across documents.')], 'tables': [{'caption': 'The Goldilocks dilemma', 'headers': ['', 'Chunks too small (<128 tok)', 'Chunks too large (>1024 tok)'], 'rows': [['Pros', 'High precision, low noise', 'Preserves narrative flow'], ['Cons', 'Missing context, fragmented facts', 'Diluted / averaged embedding'], ['Risk', 'Hallucination from missing relationships', 'Low retrieval accuracy, high token cost']]}, {'caption': 'Structural strategy comparison', 'headers': ['Strategy', 'Compute', 'Boundary integrity', 'Best for'], 'rows': [['Fixed-size', 'Very low', 'Poor', 'Fast prototypes'], ['Sentence/paragraph', 'Low', 'High', 'Narratives, articles'], ['Recursive character', 'Low-medium', 'Very high', 'Default for enterprise PDFs/web']]}, {'caption': 'Specialized pattern comparison', 'headers': ['Pattern', 'Compute cost', 'Retrieval precision', 'Best use case'], 'rows': [['Fixed-size (naive)', 'Very low', 'Low', 'Quick prototypes'], ['Parent-child', 'Medium', 'High', 'Technical docs / manuals'], ['Contextual retrieval', 'High (LLM pass)', 'Very high', 'Ambiguous enterprise PDFs'], ['Structure-aware (AST)', 'Medium', 'High', 'Code repos / API docs'], ['Agentic chunking', 'Very high', 'Maximum', 'Legal contracts / medical research'], ['Semantic chunking', 'Medium-high', 'High', 'Heterogeneous long-form narratives']]}], 'facts': ['Production baseline: 512 tokens, 10-15% overlap (50-64 tokens) gives the best hit-rate in enterprise benchmarks', "Semantic chunking mechanism: sentence split -> embed each sentence -> cosine distance between neighbors -> percentile threshold flags a 'valley' -> group into chunks", 'Contextual retrieval / semantic chunking can boost retrieval accuracy by up to +49% vs. naive fixed-character splitting', "LangChain's breakpoint_threshold_type: Percentile (default), Standard Deviation, or Interquartile Range (IQR)"], 'quirks': ['Orphan chunk problem — a chunk with no surrounding context reads as gibberish out of place; contextual retrieval fixes this by prepending a one-line explainer before embedding.'], 'qa': [('Why is recursive character splitting the industry default over naive fixed-size splitting?', 'It tries structural separators (paragraph, then line, then word) before falling back to a hard character cut, so it almost always respects natural boundaries while still guaranteeing a max chunk size.'), ("What problem does parent-child chunking solve that plain chunking doesn't?", 'It decouples the unit you search (small, precise) from the unit you feed the LLM (large, contextual) — you get precise retrieval without losing surrounding context in the answer.'), ('Describe the mechanism behind semantic chunking.', 'Split into sentences, embed each one, measure cosine distance between consecutive sentence embeddings, and cut wherever that distance spikes past a percentile threshold — that spike marks a topic change.'), ("What is 'contextual retrieval' and what problem does it fix?", 'An LLM prepends a short explanatory sentence (e.g. which document/section a chunk is from) before embedding — it fixes the orphan-chunk problem and can lift retrieval accuracy up to ~49%.')]}, {'id': 'embedding-models', 'num': '06', 'eyebrow': 'Turning text into numbers', 'title': 'Embedding Strategies & Models', 'core': 'The field evolved from sparse keyword vectors to dense neural single-vector embeddings to multi-vector token interactions to sparse+dense hybrids. Picking a model means trading dimensions, context length, price, and hosting control.', 'defs': [('One-hot vectors', 'Vector length = vocabulary size, exactly one position is 1. Simple and interpretable, but suffers the curse of dimensionality and has zero semantic relationship between words.'), ('TF-IDF', 'Term Frequency x Inverse Document Frequency — rewards words frequent in one document but rare across the corpus. Fast and transparent, but gameable by repetition and blind to synonyms.'), ('BM25 (Okapi)', 'The gold-standard sparse/lexical ranking algorithm; improves on TF-IDF with term-frequency saturation and document-length normalization.'), ('Dense embeddings', 'Compress an entire chunk into one fixed-length vector (768-1536 dims) via a neural model — capture context/intent even with zero word overlap, but are lossy on long passages and weak at exact keyword/SKU matches.'), ('Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL)', 'Lets you truncate an embedding vector (e.g. 1536->256 dims) without breaking its structure — key info is packed into the earliest dimensions. Must re-normalize after truncation.'), ('Hybrid search', 'Dense (semantic) + sparse (BM25/SPLADE, exact keyword) combined via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).')], 'tables': [{'caption': 'Sparse vs. dense embeddings', 'headers': ['', 'Sparse (BM25/TF-IDF)', 'Dense (neural)'], 'rows': [['Vector size', 'Dictionary size (50,000+ dims)', '768-1,536 dims'], ['Density', 'Mostly zeros', 'Continuous, non-zero'], ['Captures', 'Exact keyword frequency', 'Latent semantic meaning'], ['Weakness', 'Blind to synonyms', 'Weak on exact jargon/SKU matches']]}, {'caption': 'BM25 core parameters', 'headers': ['Parameter', 'Meaning', 'Typical value'], 'rows': [['k1', 'Term-frequency saturation — how much repeats matter', '1.2-2.0'], ['b', 'Length normalization strength (1.0 = full penalty, 0 = off)', '0.75']]}, {'caption': 'OpenAI embedding family', 'headers': ['Model', 'Dimensions', 'Context', 'MTEB', 'Price / 1M'], 'rows': [['text-embedding-ada-002 (legacy)', '1,536 fixed', '8,191', '61.0%', '$0.10'], ['text-embedding-3-small', '1,536 (->256 via MRL)', '8,191', '62.3%', '$0.02 ($0.01 batch)'], ['text-embedding-3-large', '3,072 (->256 via MRL)', '8,191', '64.6%', '$0.13 ($0.065 batch)']]}, {'caption': 'Google Gemini embedding family', 'headers': ['Model', 'Dimensions', 'Context', 'MTEB', 'Price'], 'rows': [['text-embedding-004 (legacy)', '768 fixed', '2,048', '~63.0', '$0.15/1M'], ['gemini-embedding-001 (flagship)', '3,072->256 (MRL)', '2,048', '~68.3 (multilingual SOTA)', '$0.20/1M ($0.075 batch)'], ['gemini-embedding-2 (multimodal)', '3,072->128 (MRL)', '8,192', '~68.2', 'text $0.20 / image $0.45 / audio $6.50 / video $12 per 1M']]}, {'caption': 'Cross-provider comparison', 'headers': ['Model', 'Dims', 'Context', 'Price/1M', 'Hosting', 'Strength'], 'rows': [['text-embedding-3-small', '1536 or 256', '8,191', '$0.02', 'Proprietary', 'Best price/perf English RAG'], ['text-embedding-3-large', '3072 or 256', '8,191', '$0.13', 'Proprietary', 'Highest general accuracy'], ['Gemini Embedding 2', '768-3072', '8,192', '$0.10', 'Google Cloud', 'Native multimodal'], ['Cohere Embed v4', '1024', '128,000', '$0.12', 'API/VPC', 'Cross-lingual, long docs'], ['Voyage-3 / Code-3', '1024', '32,000', '$0.12-0.18', 'Proprietary', 'Code / legal SOTA'], ['BGE-M3', '1024', '8,192', 'Free', 'Open source (MIT)', 'Unified dense+sparse+multi-vector']]}], 'facts': ['Worked BM25 example: a doc mentioning "dogs" twice can still score lower than a shorter doc, because the length-normalization term (b) penalizes it', 'Vector byte math: bytes = dimensions x 4 (float32) -> 1,536-dim ≈ 6 KB, 3,072-dim ≈ 12 KB, 256-dim (MRL) ≈ 1 KB', 'MRL: 1536->256 dims = ~83% storage reduction while keeping ~98% retrieval quality', 'Cohere Embed v4: 128,000-token context — largest of the major embedding APIs; natively multimodal text+image', 'Voyage-4 family shares ONE embedding space across all tiers — index with the large model, query with the lite model, no re-indexing needed', 'Qwen3-Embedding-8B is #1 on MTEB multilingual (70.6), beating every commercial API — fully open weight (Apache 2.0)', "BM25 is the default similarity in Elasticsearch, OpenSearch, Apache Lucene, and powers Anthropic's Contextual Retrieval"], 'quirks': ['One-hot vectors: cosine similarity between any two distinct words is exactly 0 (mutually orthogonal) — this is precisely why dense embeddings replaced them.', 'BM25 is still bag-of-words: "dog bites man" and "man bites dog" score identically — no word-order awareness.', 'OpenAI/most proprietary embedding APIs give no native sparse/lexical channel — pair with BM25/SPLADE for hybrid retrieval.'], 'qa': [('What two upgrades does BM25 add over plain TF-IDF?', "Term-frequency saturation (k1) so repeated words give diminishing returns, and document-length normalization (b) so a short doc mentioning a term isn't unfairly outscored by a long doc."), ('Why would you choose a hybrid (dense + sparse) retriever instead of dense alone?', 'Dense embeddings catch synonyms and paraphrase but miss exact keyword/SKU/jargon matches that sparse (BM25) methods nail — combining both via RRF covers both failure modes.'), ('What is Matryoshka Representation Learning and why does it matter operationally?', "A training technique that packs the most important signal into a vector's earliest dimensions, so you can truncate (e.g. 1536->256 dims) for cheaper storage/faster search while retaining most retrieval quality — must re-normalize after truncation."), ('When would you pick an open-weight embedding model (BGE-M3, Qwen3) over an API?', 'Data sovereignty / HIPAA or air-gapped requirements, zero per-call cost at scale, or needing a single model that natively does dense+sparse+multi-vector retrieval.')]}, {'id': 'vector-databases', 'num': '07', 'eyebrow': 'Storing and searching meaning', 'title': 'Vector Databases', 'core': 'Vector databases store data as high-dimensional numeric vectors and specialize in similarity search (nearest neighbors) rather than exact row/column matching. At scale, exact search is too slow, so Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) algorithms trade a sliver of accuracy for major speed.', 'defs': [('FAISS', "Meta's open-source similarity-search library (not a full DB) — extremely fast, free, fine-tunable, but no built-in persistence, CRUD, auth, or horizontal scaling."), ('IVF (Inverted File Index)', "Uses k-means to partition vectors into nlist Voronoi cells; search compares the query only to the nprobe closest cells' posting lists instead of everything."), ('HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World)', 'A multi-layer graph — sparse top layer for long jumps, dense base layer for fine search — giving logarithmic-time, high-recall search.'), ('Product Quantization (PQ)', 'Lossy compression: splits each vector into sub-vectors, replaces each with a 1-byte centroid ID from a trained codebook — up to 64x compression.'), ('Asymmetric Distance Computation (ADC)', 'PQ search trick: the query stays full-precision; a lookup table approximates distance to each compressed database vector without decompressing it.'), ('ChromaDB', 'Easiest way to stand up a RAG prototype (<10 lines of code) — HNSW for vectors + SQLite for metadata, embedded or client-server.'), ('Pinecone', 'Fully-managed, cloud-native vector DB — zero infra, auto-scaling, but SaaS-only with no self-hosting and no relational joins/ACID.'), ('Weaviate', 'Open-source, stores objects and vectors together; native hybrid search (HNSW + BM25 fused via RRF).'), ('Qdrant', 'Open-source, written in Rust; in-traversal metadata filtering avoids the accuracy loss of pre/post filtering.'), ('Milvus', 'Open-source, cloud-native, handles 10B+ vectors via a decoupled compute/storage microservice architecture on Kubernetes.'), ('pgvector', 'PostgreSQL extension adding a native vector type + HNSW/IVFFlat indexes — zero ETL sync and full ACID, but a lower scale ceiling (~10M vectors).')], 'tables': [{'caption': 'FAISS index family performance', 'headers': ['Index', 'Recall', 'Memory vs. flat', 'Speed'], 'rows': [['IndexFlatL2 (brute force)', '100%', 'Baseline (100%)', 'Instant to build, slow to search'], ['IndexIVFFlat', '95-99%', '~100%', 'Fast build'], ['IndexIVFPQ', '80-92%', '5-10%', 'Very high speed'], ['IndexIVFSQ', '92-97%', '~25%', 'High speed'], ['IndexHNSWPQ', '90-98%', '15-20%', 'Ultra-high speed, slow build']]}, {'caption': 'HNSW vs IVFFlat indexing (pgvector)', 'headers': ['', 'HNSW', 'IVFFlat'], 'rows': [['Recall / speed', 'Highest', 'Lower with fewer probes'], ['RAM / build time', 'Higher', 'Lighter, faster build'], ['Training required', 'No', 'Yes']]}, {'caption': 'Vector DB landscape', 'headers': ['Feature', 'Milvus', 'Pinecone', 'FAISS', 'pgvector'], 'rows': [['License', 'Apache 2.0', 'Proprietary SaaS', 'Open-source library', 'Open-source extension'], ['Architecture', 'Distributed microservices', 'Managed cloud pods', 'Single-node C++ lib', 'Relational extension'], ['Scale', '10B+', 'High', 'Limited by memory', 'Small/medium (~10M)'], ['Hybrid search', 'Native', 'Yes', 'No', 'Basic SQL filters']]}], 'facts': ['RAM math (FAISS flat): 1M vectors x 768 dims x 4 bytes = 3.072 GB; 1B vectors x 1,536 dims ≈ 6.1 TB', 'PQ compression: a 128-dim float32 vector (512 bytes) compresses to 8 bytes with PQ8 — 64x compression, 98.4% RAM reduction', 'HNSW key params: M (max connections/node, 8-64), ef_build, ef_search (tunable speed/recall trade-off)', 'IVF typically cuts comparisons by ~95% vs. brute force by only searching the nprobe closest of nlist clusters', 'FAISS flat search recommended only under ~100,000 vectors — used as the ground-truth benchmark baseline for ANN methods', "Qdrant's Product Quantization gives up to 64x RAM compression while staying self-hosted and Rust-fast"], 'quirks': ["IVF boundary problem — a true nearest neighbor sitting just across a Voronoi cell border can be missed if that cell isn't in the top-nprobe.", 'HNSW cost — best recall/latency trade-off available, but it needs the raw vectors + graph edges fully in RAM and is slow to build.', 'FAISS is a library, not a database — no persistence, auth, or multi-tenancy out of the box; you build that layer yourself.'], 'qa': [('Why do production vector search systems use ANN instead of exact search?', 'Exact (brute-force) search is O(N*d) — linear in dataset size — so at millions/billions of vectors it becomes too slow; ANN algorithms like HNSW or IVF trade a small, tunable amount of recall for orders-of-magnitude faster search.'), ('Explain how HNSW achieves logarithmic search time.', 'It builds a multi-layer graph — a sparse top layer lets you jump long distances quickly, and each lower layer is denser, so search greedily descends layer by layer, refining to the nearest neighbor only at the base layer, similar to a skip list.'), ('When would you choose pgvector over a dedicated vector DB like Milvus or Pinecone?', 'When your data already lives in Postgres and scale stays under roughly 10M vectors — you get zero ETL/sync overhead and full ACID/SQL joins, at the cost of a lower scale ceiling.'), ("What is Product Quantization and what's the trade-off?", 'It compresses each vector into a small set of 1-byte centroid IDs from a trained codebook, shrinking memory up to 64x — the trade-off is quantization error (lossy) and a mandatory training step, so it pays off mainly above ~100k vectors.')]}, {'id': 'metadata-filtering', 'num': '08', 'eyebrow': 'Narrow before you search', 'title': 'Metadata Filtering in RAG', 'core': 'Structured tags (department, date, author, access level) narrow the candidate pool before running expensive vector math — improving precision, cutting latency, and enforcing access control at retrieval time.', 'defs': [('Pre-filtering', 'Filter before distance calculation. Guarantees constraint compliance and cuts compute, but risks returning fewer than K results if the filtered set is small.'), ('Post-filtering', 'Retrieve top-K first, then discard non-matches. Risks low recall — you may end up with far fewer than K usable results.'), ('Self-querying (automated query parsing)', 'An LLM parses a natural-language question into a vector query + a structured filter automatically.'), ('Single-stage / in-traversal filtering', 'Metadata filters applied during graph traversal rather than before/after — avoids the recall loss of pre/post filtering.')], 'tables': [{'caption': 'Filtering strategy trade-offs', 'headers': ['Strategy', 'Precision', 'Latency', 'Best for'], 'rows': [['Pre-filtering', 'Very high', 'Low', 'RBAC / access control'], ['Post-filtering', 'Variable / can be low', 'Higher (wasted search)', 'Small, static datasets'], ['Single-stage hybrid', 'High', 'Very low', 'Production vector DBs'], ['LLM self-query', 'Extremely high', '+~150ms overhead', 'Complex multi-attribute queries']]}], 'facts': ['Filtering can cut latency by ~90% (searching 120 candidates vs. 10,000+) and boost precision by >5x', 'Metadata categories: structural (filename, page, chunk ID), LLM-generated (summary, entities, sentiment tags), security (tenant ID, ACLs, classification)'], 'quirks': [], 'qa': [('Why is pre-filtering generally preferred over post-filtering?', 'Pre-filtering shrinks the candidate set before the expensive similarity search, guaranteeing constraint compliance and lower compute — post-filtering wastes work retrieving top-K first and can leave you with fewer than K relevant results.'), ('What is self-querying and when is it worth the extra latency?', 'An LLM converts a natural-language question into a vector query plus a structured metadata filter automatically — worth it for complex, multi-attribute questions, despite the ~150ms LLM-call overhead.')]}, {'id': 'reranking', 'num': '09', 'eyebrow': 'A second, smarter pass', 'title': 'Re-ranking', 'core': 'Embedding similarity search is fast but approximate. A re-ranker reads the query and each candidate together for a far more precise relevance score — worth the extra cost on a short list (~10-50 candidates).', 'defs': [('Bi-encoder', 'Encodes query and document separately, then compares vectors (e.g. cosine). Cheap, pre-computable, used for first-pass broad retrieval.'), ('Cross-encoder', 'Feeds the query and ONE document jointly through the model with full cross-attention, outputting a single relevance score. Accurate but must run live per pair.'), ('ColBERT', 'Keeps a separate embedding per token (not one vector per doc); each query token is matched against every doc token via MaxSim. A middle ground — faster than cross-encoders.'), ('Cohere Rerank', 'Managed reranking API — send a query + candidate list, get back a reordered list with relevance scores. Zero infra, but adds network latency and sends data to the cloud.'), ('LLM-as-reranker', "Use a general-purpose LLM's own reasoning to score/rank candidates — handles negation and multi-hop inference well, but is the slowest and most expensive option.")], 'tables': [{'caption': 'Bi-encoder vs. cross-encoder', 'headers': ['Feature', 'Bi-encoder', 'Cross-encoder'], 'rows': [['Input', 'Encoded separately', 'Encoded jointly'], ['Pre-computation', 'Yes (DB vectors saved)', 'No — must run live per pair'], ['Attention', 'None during encoding', 'Full cross-attention'], ['Latency', '~5-15ms', '~100-500ms for a candidate set'], ['Role', 'First-pass broad retrieval', 'Second-pass precision re-ranking']]}, {'caption': 'Choosing a re-ranking approach', 'headers': ['Approach', 'Speed', 'Cost', 'Accuracy', 'Setup'], 'rows': [['Cross-encoder', 'Slow', 'Free (self-hosted)', 'Very high', 'Moderate'], ['ColBERT', 'Medium', 'Free (self-hosted)', 'High', 'Higher (special indexing)'], ['LLM-as-reranker', 'Slowest', 'High', 'High', 'Easiest'], ['Cohere Rerank', 'Fast', 'Paid API', 'Very high', 'Easiest']]}], 'facts': ['Two-stage hybrid pattern: Stage 1 (bi-encoder/BM25) narrows 1M+ docs -> 50 candidates in ~10ms; Stage 2 (cross-encoder) re-ranks 50 -> top 3 in ~80ms', '3 LLM-reranking paradigms: Pointwise (score each independently, parallelizable), Pairwise (compare two at a time, O(N^2)), Listwise (RankGPT — one prompt outputs the full ranked order)', 'LLM-as-reranker can lift top-10 precision (NDCG@10) by up to +15% over standard cross-encoders', 'Rule of thumb: run a cross-encoder or Cohere Rerank on the top 10-50 vector-search results, never on the full corpus'], 'quirks': ["Cross-encoders can't be pre-indexed at all — every query x document pair needs a fresh forward pass, so they only scale to tens/hundreds of candidates, never millions."], 'qa': [("Why can't you use a cross-encoder as your primary retriever over millions of documents?", "It requires a live joint forward pass for every query-document pair — there's no way to pre-compute document embeddings, so cost scales linearly with corpus size at query time."), ("What does ColBERT offer that a plain bi-encoder or a cross-encoder doesn't?", 'Token-level (not document-level) matching via MaxSim, giving finer-grained relevance than a bi-encoder while still allowing document token embeddings to be pre-computed and cached.'), ('Describe the standard two-stage retrieval + re-ranking pipeline.', 'A fast, cheap retriever (BM25 or a bi-encoder) narrows the full corpus down to ~50 candidates, then a slower, more accurate re-ranker (cross-encoder, ColBERT, or an LLM) re-orders just those 50 to surface the true top-3-5.')]}, {'id': 'hybrid-retrieval', 'num': '10', 'eyebrow': 'Best of both signals', 'title': 'Hybrid Retrieval Architecture', 'core': 'Combines exact keyword search (BM25) with meaning-based vector search, typically merged with Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) — covering both the synonym gap of sparse search and the jargon/SKU gap of dense search.', 'defs': [('Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)', "Merges two separately-ranked result lists (e.g. BM25 ranks + vector-search ranks) into one combined ranking, without needing the two systems' scores to be on the same scale.")], 'tables': [], 'facts': [], 'quirks': [], 'qa': [('Why is RRF a good fusion method for hybrid search?', "BM25 scores and cosine-similarity scores live on completely different scales, so averaging raw scores is meaningless — RRF fuses based on each result's rank position in each list instead, which is scale-independent.")]}, {'id': 'rag-evaluation', 'num': '11', 'eyebrow': 'Proving it works', 'title': 'RAG Evaluation', 'core': 'RAG fails in exactly two places: the retriever fetches the wrong knowledge, or the generator writes an inaccurate answer from the right knowledge. Evaluation exists to pinpoint which stage broke.', 'defs': [('Context Relevance', 'Is the retrieved context actually relevant to the query? (Retriever quality.)'), ('Groundedness / Faithfulness', 'Is the answer strictly supported by the retrieved context, claim-by-claim? (0.0-1.0.)'), ('Answer Relevance', 'Does the answer actually address what was asked?'), ('Context Precision', 'Of the retrieved chunks, how many were actually relevant — minimizes clutter.'), ('Context Recall', 'Did retrieval capture everything needed, checked against ground truth.'), ('LLM-as-a-Judge', 'Using a strong LLM (GPT-4, Claude) as an automated evaluator — correlates with human experts >85% of the time.'), ('Perplexity (PPL)', "Quantifies how 'surprised' a model is by the next token. PPL=K means the model is choosing among ~K equally likely options at each step."), ('BLEU', 'N-gram precision metric from machine translation — penalizes both mismatched n-grams and artificially short answers (brevity penalty).'), ('ROUGE', 'Recall-oriented n-gram overlap metric (ROUGE-1 unigram, ROUGE-2 bigram, ROUGE-L longest common subsequence) — favors summarization tasks.')], 'tables': [{'caption': 'RAG Triad', 'headers': ['Leg', 'Question it answers'], 'rows': [['Context Relevance', 'Was the retrieved context relevant to the query?'], ['Groundedness', 'Is the answer strictly supported by the context?'], ['Answer Relevance', 'Does the answer address what was actually asked?']]}, {'caption': 'Evaluation framework comparison', 'headers': ['Framework', 'Core focus', 'Best for'], 'rows': [['Ragas', 'Component evaluation (faithfulness, context recall/precision)', 'Development & experimentation'], ['TruLens', 'Live RAG Triad feedback functions on real traces', 'Interactive dashboarding'], ['DeepEval', "'Pytest for LLMs' — unit-test style scoring", 'CI/CD automated pipelines'], ['Arize Phoenix / LangSmith', 'Tracing & observability (latency, drift, spans)', 'Production monitoring']]}, {'caption': 'BLEU vs. ROUGE vs. LLM-as-Judge', 'headers': ['Metric', 'Focus', 'Needs reference?', 'Catches hallucination?', 'Cost'], 'rows': [['BLEU', 'Precision (n-gram overlap)', 'Yes', 'No', 'Free'], ['ROUGE', 'Recall (n-gram overlap)', 'Yes', 'No', 'Free'], ['LLM-as-Judge (RAGAS)', 'Semantic faithfulness', 'No (reference-free)', 'Yes', 'API tokens']]}], 'facts': ['4-step evaluation workflow: curate 30-50 query/context pairs -> evaluate retrieval (precision/recall) -> evaluate generation (faithfulness) -> automate as CI/CD threshold guards', 'TruLens groundedness benchmark: 0.81 F1, outperforming custom fine-tuned classifiers', 'Low perplexity (1-15) = confident/predictable text; high perplexity (>50) = struggling with ambiguity', 'Key trap: low perplexity does NOT equal factually correct — confident hallucinations are still hallucinations, so PPL must be paired with a faithfulness/groundedness metric'], 'quirks': ['BLEU/ROUGE can score a hallucinated answer highly if it happens to share n-grams with the reference — neither actually checks factual correctness.', "LLM-as-a-Judge inherits the judge model's own biases, so evaluator choice matters as much as the metric."], 'qa': [("What's the difference between context recall and context precision?", 'Context recall asks whether retrieval captured everything needed (compared to ground truth); context precision asks how much of what was retrieved was actually relevant.'), ('Why is faithfulness a generation metric and not a retrieval metric?', 'It measures whether every claim in the generated answer is actually supported by the retrieved context — a failure here means the generator hallucinated even though the right context may have been retrieved.'), ("Why can't you rely on BLEU or ROUGE alone to evaluate a RAG system?", 'Both are lexical n-gram overlap metrics — they can score a fluent, hallucinated answer highly if it shares words with the reference, and they penalize correct paraphrases. Neither checks groundedness.'), ('What does it mean that LLM-as-a-Judge correlates with human experts >85% of the time, and why does that matter?', "It means an LLM judge is a reasonably reliable stand-in for expensive human evaluation at scale — enough to automate RAG evaluation in CI/CD, but the residual disagreement means it shouldn't be the only signal for high-stakes decisions.")]}, {'id': 'llm-families', 'num': '12', 'eyebrow': "Who's building the models", 'title': 'LLM Model Families', 'core': 'Every major lab trades off context length, cost, openness, and specialization differently — know the shape of each family, not just one model name.', 'defs': [('GPT (OpenAI)', 'GPT-3 introduced in-context few-shot learning; GPT-4/4o added native multimodality and agentic tool calling; GPT-5 adds a real-time router between instant and deep chain-of-thought reasoning.'), ('Gemini (Google)', 'Pioneered multi-million-token context (Gemini 1.5 Pro: 2,097,152 tokens) with near-100% needle-in-haystack recall; context caching saves up to 75% on repeated input.'), ('Claude (Anthropic)', 'Three tiers — Opus (max intelligence), Sonnet (balanced workhorse), Haiku (fast/cheap). All modern models: 200,000-token context; Constitutional AI alignment; SOTA on SWE-Bench coding.'), ('Llama (Meta)', 'Open-weights, permissive license, base for thousands of fine-tunes; Llama 3.1 405B was the first open model rivaling closed frontier LLMs.'), ('Mistral', 'European sovereignty focus; Mixtral 8x7B is a Sparse Mixture-of-Experts — only 12.9B of 46.7B total params active per token, ~6x faster inference than a 70B dense model at similar quality.'), ('DeepSeek', 'Ultra-efficient open weights; Sparse MoE + Multi-Head Latent Attention (MLA) compress the KV cache; R1 showed reasoning can emerge from pure reinforcement learning.'), ('Grok (xAI)', 'Trained on the Colossus supercluster (200,000+ H100 GPUs); real-time X/Twitter data integration bypasses static knowledge cutoffs.')], 'tables': [{'caption': 'GPT family evolution', 'headers': ['Model', 'Params', 'Context', 'Key advance'], 'rows': [['GPT-1 (2018)', '117M', '512 tokens', 'Unsupervised pretrain + supervised fine-tune paradigm'], ['GPT-2', '1.5B', '1,024 tokens', 'Zero-shot task transfer'], ['GPT-3 (2020)', '175B', '2,048 tokens', 'In-context few-shot learning'], ['GPT-4 / 4o', '~1T (MoE)', 'up to 128k', 'Native multimodality, agentic tool calling'], ['GPT-5 (2025-26)', 'Frontier system', '400k-1M+', 'Real-time instant-vs-deep-reasoning router']]}, {'caption': 'Transformer families across labs', 'headers': ['Architecture', 'Key mechanism', 'Examples', 'Primary tasks'], 'rows': [['Encoder-only', 'Bidirectional attention', 'BERT, RoBERTa', 'Classification, search, sentiment'], ['Decoder-only', 'Masked causal attention', 'GPT-4, Llama, Claude', 'Text generation, chatbots, code'], ['Encoder-decoder', 'Cross-attention', 'T5, BART, Whisper', 'Translation, summarization, audio']]}], 'facts': ['GPT-4o cut input cost >12x vs. GPT-4 ($2.50 vs $30.00 per 1M tokens)', 'Claude: prompt caching cuts latency up to 85% and cost up to 90% for repeated long documents', 'Llama VRAM needs: 8B model ≈ 6-10GB (one RTX 4090); 70B ≈ 40-140GB; 405B ≈ 430-810GB+', 'DeepSeek-V3 full pre-training: <2.8M H800 GPU hours (~$5.6M) — API pricing ~$0.14-$0.55/1M input tokens, 10-20x cheaper than closed frontier models', 'Grok context scales up to 1,000,000 tokens (Grok 4.3/4.5); Grok 4.3 cut output cost >80% vs. Grok 3', 'Mixtral 8x22B: 141B total params, only 39B active per token, native function calling, 64k context, Apache 2.0'], 'quirks': ['MoE (Mixture-of-Experts) models like Mixtral/DeepSeek still need enough VRAM to load every expert even though only a subset activates per token — memory savings come from compute, not storage.', 'Deep-reasoning modes (GPT-5, Grok Think Mode, DeepSeek-R1) trade higher time-to-first-token for better answers — a real latency/quality dial, not free lunch.'], 'qa': [("What's the core architectural idea behind Mixtral's Sparse Mixture-of-Experts?", "Each token is routed to only a small subset of 'expert' sub-networks (e.g. 2 of 8), so total parameter count is large (46.7B) but active compute per token is small (12.9B) — near-dense-quality output at a fraction of the inference cost."), ('Why did DeepSeek-R1 matter for the field beyond just being cheap?', 'It demonstrated (via R1-Zero) that long chain-of-thought reasoning can emerge purely from reinforcement learning, without heavy supervised fine-tuning — and the reasoning traces could then be distilled into much smaller dense models.'), ("What's the practical benefit of Claude/Gemini's prompt caching?", 'When you send the same long prefix repeatedly, the model can reuse cached key/value computations instead of reprocessing it — cutting latency and cost dramatically for repeated-context workloads.')]}, {'id': 'transformer-architecture', 'num': '13', 'eyebrow': 'The engine underneath', 'title': 'Transformer Architecture', 'core': '"Attention Is All You Need" (Google, 2017) replaced sequential RNN/LSTM processing with a parallelizable, attention-based architecture — the mechanical foundation every model above is built on.', 'defs': [('Self-attention', 'Calculates how much every token should attend to every other token — resolves "The animal didn\'t cross the street because it was too tired" -> "it" = animal, not street.'), ('Query / Key / Value (Q/K/V)', 'A database analogy: Query = what a token is looking for; Key = what every token advertises; Value = the content actually pulled when a Query matches a Key via dot product.'), ('Multi-head attention', "Q/K/V are split into parallel 'heads,' each specializing in a different relationship type (grammar, pronoun reference, spatial/temporal position)."), ('Positional encoding', 'Injects word order into embeddings (without it, "Dog bites man" = "Man bites dog"). The original paper used sine/cosine functions at varying frequencies.'), ('Residual connections (Add & Norm)', 'Skip-connections around each attention/FFN layer that prevent vanishing gradients and stabilize very deep networks.'), ('Feed-Forward Network (FFN)', "A point-wise MLP that refines each token's representation independently after attention."), ('Masked self-attention', "A causal mask that hides future tokens during generation so the decoder can't 'cheat' by seeing what comes next.")], 'tables': [{'caption': 'RNN/LSTM vs. Transformer', 'headers': ['', 'RNN/LSTM', 'Transformer'], 'rows': [['Processing', 'Sequential, word-by-word', 'Parallel, all tokens at once'], ['Scaling', 'Slow to train on GPUs', 'Massively parallel scaling'], ['Long-range context', 'Forgets distant relationships', 'Direct any-token-to-any-token connection via attention']]}], 'facts': ['Encoder = input sequence -> rich contextual representation; Decoder = representation -> output sequence, generated token-by-token, autoregressively', 'Self-attention + FFN blocks repeat 12 to 96+ times in deep modern models', 'Final step every generation: Linear layer + Softmax -> a probability distribution over the entire vocabulary -> pick (or sample) the next token, repeat'], 'quirks': [], 'qa': [('Walk through the Q/K/V mechanism in one sentence.', "Every token emits a Query (what it's looking for) and a Key (what it offers); attention weight is the dot product of a Query against every Key, and that weight decides how much of each token's Value gets blended into the output."), ('Why do transformers need positional encoding at all if attention already relates every token to every other token?', 'Self-attention on its own is permutation-invariant — it has no concept of order — so without an explicit positional signal, word order would not matter to the model.'), ('What problem do residual (skip) connections solve in a 96-layer transformer?', 'They let gradients flow directly around each layer during backpropagation, preventing vanishing gradients and making it possible to train networks that deep without them destabilizing.'), ('Why is masking necessary in a decoder but not an encoder?', "A decoder generates autoregressively and must not be able to see tokens it hasn't produced yet — the causal mask blocks attention to future positions. An encoder processes the whole input at once and benefits from full bidirectional attention.")]}]

RAPID_FIRE = [("What's the difference between a token and a word?", 'A token is a model-vocabulary unit (subword, whole word, or punctuation); roughly 1 word ≈ 1.33 tokens in English.'), ('Name the three classic subword tokenizers.', 'BPE (GPT), WordPiece (BERT), SentencePiece (whitespace-agnostic, e.g. Japanese).'), ('Why does context length scale compute quadratically?', 'Self-attention scores every token against every other token — O(n^2) pairs.'), ('What is cosine similarity actually computing?', 'The cosine of the angle between two vectors — a scale-invariant similarity score from -1 to 1.'), ('Sparse vs dense embeddings — one line each.', 'Sparse (BM25): mostly-zero, exact keyword match. Dense: compact neural vector, captures meaning/synonyms.'), ("What does BM25's 'b' parameter control?", "Document length normalization — how much a long document's term counts get discounted."), ('What is Reciprocal Rank Fusion used for?', 'Merging two differently-scaled ranked lists (e.g. BM25 + vector search) into one combined ranking.'), ("Define 'chunking' in one sentence.", 'Splitting a large document into smaller coherent pieces so it fits embedding/context limits without losing meaning.'), ("What's the 'orphan chunk' problem?", 'A retrieved chunk that makes no sense without the surrounding document context — fixed by contextual retrieval.'), ("Parent-child chunking — what's the parent for, what's the child for?", "Child (small) chunks are searched for precision; parent (large) chunks are what's actually shown to the LLM for context."), ('What is HNSW in one sentence?', 'A multi-layer graph index that gives logarithmic-time approximate nearest-neighbor search.'), ("What does the 'nprobe' parameter control in IVF?", 'How many of the nearest clusters (out of nlist) are actually searched — higher = more accurate, slower.'), ('What is Product Quantization used for?', 'Compressing vectors into small per-subvector centroid codes to massively cut memory at some accuracy cost.'), ('Bi-encoder vs cross-encoder — which scales to millions of documents?', 'Bi-encoder — because document vectors are pre-computed; cross-encoders must run live per pair.'), ("What does a re-ranker add that vector search alone doesn't?", 'Full cross-attention between the query and each candidate for a much more precise relevance score, on a short list.'), ('Name the three legs of the RAG Triad.', 'Context relevance, groundedness/faithfulness, answer relevance.'), ("What's the difference between context precision and context recall?", 'Precision = how much of what was retrieved is relevant; recall = whether everything relevant was retrieved.'), ('Why is faithfulness a generation-side metric?', "It checks whether the LLM's claims are actually supported by the retrieved context — a failure means hallucination, not bad retrieval."), ('What does a low perplexity score tell you — and not tell you?', 'The model was confident/predictable in its token choices; it does NOT mean the output is factually correct.'), ('BLEU is precision-based; ROUGE is ___-based.', 'Recall-based.'), ('What is Matryoshka Representation Learning good for?', 'Truncating an embedding to fewer dimensions (cheaper storage/search) while keeping most of its retrieval quality.'), ('What is a Mixture-of-Experts model, briefly?', 'A model where only a subset of specialized sub-networks activate per token, keeping compute low despite a large total parameter count.'), ('Encoder-only vs decoder-only vs encoder-decoder — one example and one use each.', 'BERT/classification (encoder-only); GPT/generation (decoder-only); T5/translation (encoder-decoder).'), ("Why can't self-attention alone tell word order?", "It's permutation-invariant by default — positional encoding has to be added explicitly."), ('What is masked self-attention for?', 'Blocking a decoder from attending to future tokens during autoregressive generation.'), ("What causes the 'lost in the middle' effect?", 'Models attend best to the start and end of a long context, so information placed in the middle is more likely to be under-used.'), ('When should you prefer RAG over stuffing a giant context window?', 'When cost/latency matter, the corpus exceeds even a huge context window, or you need traceable source citations.'), ("What's the key risk of post-filtering metadata after vector search?", 'You can end up with fewer than K usable results if most of the top-K get filtered out after the fact.'), ('Give one reason to pick pgvector over a dedicated vector DB.', 'Your data already lives in Postgres and scale is under ~10M vectors — you avoid ETL/sync and keep ACID + SQL joins.'), ("What's the practical difference between TF-IDF and BM25?", "BM25 adds term-frequency saturation and document-length normalization on top of TF-IDF's raw frequency x rarity.")]

NUMBERS = [('1 word ≈ tokens', '1.33 tokens'), ('1 token ≈ words / chars', '0.75 words / ~4 characters'), ('Typical LLM vocabulary size', '32,000-100,000 tokens'), ('Context growth over a few years', '~1,000x'), ('Compute cost of doubling context (4k->8k)', '~4x (quadratic self-attention)'), ('Dense embedding dimensions (typical)', '768-1,536+'), ('Production chunk size baseline', '512 tokens, 10-15% overlap'), ('Semantic/contextual chunking accuracy lift', 'up to +49%'), ('Chunk size danger zone (too small)', '< 128 tokens'), ('Chunk size danger zone (too large)', '> 1,024 tokens'), ('BM25 k1 (term-frequency saturation)', 'typically 1.2-2.0'), ('BM25 b (length normalization)', 'typically 0.75'), ('MRL truncation (1536->256 dims) storage saved', '~83% saved, ~98% quality kept'), ('FAISS flat-search recommended scale', '< 100,000 vectors'), ('Product Quantization compression', 'up to 64x'), ('Cohere Embed v4 context window', '128,000 tokens'), ('Claude context window (modern models)', '200,000 tokens'), ('Gemini 1.5 Pro context window', '2,097,152 tokens (2M)'), ('Grok 4.3/4.5 context window', '1,000,000 tokens'), ('Claude prompt caching savings', 'up to 85% latency, 90% cost'), ('Transformer block repeats in deep models', '12-96+ layers'), ('LLM-as-Judge agreement with human experts', '> 85%'), ('TruLens groundedness benchmark', '0.81 F1'), ('Confident text perplexity range', '1-15'), ('Struggling/ambiguous text perplexity', '> 50')]

